   209	### 2.2 What the bundle never supplies
   210	
   211	The bundle never supplies the trust configuration, δ, ε, k or any verifier policy. [A9:154; SPEC R-5.9:1219]
   212	
   213	## 3. Verifier dispatch, stages and precedence
   214	
   215	### 3.1 Dispatch
   216	
   217	1. The verifier reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first. An unrecognized label gives
   218	   `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing else is evaluated. [ENC §8:742-743; A9:287]
   219	2. Out-of-profile layer structure or request gives the same result without evaluation. [DD:70; A9:287]
   220	   Cases c (two-layer transplant) and h (renewal wrapper) land here. [A9:179, 185]
   221	3. **Dispatch reads exactly three things**, in order, and nothing else [OWNER 2026-10-14, G17]:
   222	   - **D1.** The bytes are not strict JSON (ENC §1.4 checks 1–5), or exceed 2^27 bytes: `UNVERIFIABLE /
   223	     OUT_OF_PROFILE`.
   224	   - **D2.** Top-level `profile` absent or not exactly `tessera-v1-strict`: same.
   225	   - **D3.** `layers` not an array of length 1: same (cases c and h).
   226	   - **D4.** The request policy is a **verifier input**, never a bundle member; its only value is `strict`. Any other
   227	     request: `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, detail `REQUEST_UNSUPPORTED`, with no evaluation (case d1's waiver
   228	     refused).
   229	   *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` what exactly dispatch inspects … Decide the fields dispatch reads, and where the request
   230	   type lives", 2026-10-13.)*
   231	4. Once dispatched to `tessera-v1-strict`, every codec rejection is `INVALID`. That includes a signed `profile` other
   232	   than `tessera-v1-strict`, an unknown tag and an unknown canonicalization version. [ENC §8:744-746]
   233	5. The one **codec-level** in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` that concerns the bytes is a well-formed unknown `alg`.
   234	   [ENC §8:747-748; SPEC R-2.37:308] The profile adds further in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` outcomes (missing members,
   235	   unknown spec hash, binding forms, and the evidence outcomes below); they are not turned `INVALID` by this
   236	   sentence. [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-contra] *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: previously "The one in-profile
   237	   `UNVERIFIABLE` …", repeating ENC §8's stale wording, which G01 already requires to say "codec-level".)*
   238	
   239	### 3.2 Stages
   240	
   241	The stage names follow the spec's. The spec registers no order between stages, only that the verdict does not
   242	depend on evaluation order. [SPEC:1237-1248; SPEC R-5.16:1250] There are ten stages after dispatch. The
   243	table presents ENC/SPEC material; it is not a newly fixed execution order (section 0.1, item 6).
   244	
   245	| Stage | What V1 does | Source |
   246	|---|---|---|
   247	| (a) Parse | Decode every frame, the manifest and `A_sig` under ENC §1 to §5. Reason codes are reported in ENC's order. | [ENC §1.4:172-190; ENC §3.1:241-248; ENC §5:385-394] |
   248	| (b) Type | Tag must be in T and be the expected one: `TAG_UNKNOWN`, `TAG_MISMATCH`. No wrapper stage: one layer only. | [ENC §2:215-218; A9:161] |
   249	| (c) Algorithm | A well-formed unknown `common.alg` gives `UNVERIFIABLE`. A malformed, missing or substituted id gives `INVALID`. | [ENC §3.2:276; SPEC V13-V15:1282-1284] |
   250	| (d) Key binding | Each presented key hashes to its slot's manifest fingerprint (`KEY_MISMATCH`). `manifest_sha256` equals the hash of the manifest frame. | [ENC §5.3:509; ENC §3.2:280] |
   251	| (e) Authority | DNS channel and git channel (section 6), the issuance window, possession proofs. | [A9:143; A9:419-421] |
   252	| (f) Signature set | Both slots are required, from the signed manifest, never from what the bundle presents. Common bytes are byte-equal across slots. | [SPEC V28-V31:1327-1330; ENC §3.4:303-320] |
   253	| (g) Integrity | `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. Each committed `S` verifies over its committed `F`. Any difference from the committed tuple gives `RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`; a bad signature gives `SIG_INVALID`. | [ENC §5.3:498-511] |
   254	| (h) Temporal | The three-conjunct predicate at k = 6, plus the T rule (section 7). | [A9:144; SPEC V33-V36:1342-1346; SPEC R-5.19:1352] |
   255	| (i) Revocation | Not evaluated. The report says so. | [A9:255-258] |
   256	| (j) Partition | Section 3.3. | [SPEC R-5.25:1368] |
   257	
   258	- The issuance-window check is part of stage (e) (C4). [A9:419-421; ENC §7.1:636-643]
   259	- **Reported reason.** The reported reason is the first failed check in the **total check order** of section 15.7;
   260	  if none failed, the first unperformable check in that order. The order is a reporting order, not an execution
   261	  order. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G31] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` which reason code is reported when several different
   262	  checks fail", 2026-10-13.)*
   263	- **`DNS_*` record class.** A TXT RRset that is present is run through ENC §7's fifteen-step order; every failure is
   264	  `INVALID` with ENC's code, whether or not DNSSEC was performable. An absent TXT group is `UNVERIFIABLE /
   265	  DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`. ENC's `DNS_RRSET` zero case stays a codec vector, unreachable from a V1 container.
   266	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G24] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict class of the `DNS_*` record rejections", 2026-10-13.)*
   267	- **Size gates.** An empty snapshot body or a payload over its cap gives `LENGTH_CAP`, before the JSON categories.
   268	  The gates are exact and separate: container 2^27 bytes, payload per ENC §1, snapshot body 2^24. [OWNER 2026-10-14,
   269	  G30] For an empty snapshot body this profile governs over ENC §6's `1 ≤ len` wording and over
   270	  `formal/p8/vectors/gen.py` (`decode_snapshot`, which reports `SCHEMA`): the outcome is `INVALID / LENGTH_CAP`.
   271	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-20]
   272	
   273	### 3.3 Precedence and verdicts
   274	
   275	- Order of decision: any required check failed gives `INVALID`; else any required check unperformable gives
   276	  `UNVERIFIABLE`; else `VALID_STRICT`. There is no waiver set, so the degraded branch never applies. [SPEC
   277	  R-5.25:1368; A9:161]
   278	- `INVALID` dominates `UNVERIFIABLE`. [A9:182; SPEC R-5.26:1370; SPEC R-2.33:300] Case d3 depends on it.
   279	- The unperformable rule applies to required checks only. [SPEC R-2.32:298]
   280	- A verdict is never a bare boolean. `UNVERIFIABLE` is never promoted. [SPEC R-2.34:302; SPEC R-5.38:1394]
   281	- No fallback, negotiation or normalization during verification. [SPEC R-5.18:1266; ENC §1.2:156-158]
   282	- **Aggregation.** Physical evaluation order is unrestricted, but reporting uses the fixed total check order (section
   283	  15.7). Early termination is permitted only when every earlier-priority reportable check is settled and every
   284	  stable field, including T, is determined. A failure prevents only checks whose own necessary input cannot be
   285	  obtained; it does not suppress independent receipt-anchor evaluation needed for T. Derived checks are unperformable
   286	  when necessary inputs cannot be obtained, and never pass by default. ~~An unknown `alg` routes the slot as opaque
   287	  (Lemma 7a)~~: signature and possession semantics under an `alg` other than the catalog-supported id are
   288	  unperformable; independent schema and hash checks still run. Codec routing is section 1's codec-known versus
   289	  policy-supported rule. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G26; r2-F01] *Dated correction 2026-10-14: the resolutions draft's "a
   290	  verifier may stop early only on a failed check" is withdrawn; it let two schedules report different stable
   291	  fields. Dated correction 2026-10-14 (r2-F01): "an unknown `alg` routes the slot as opaque" is withdrawn; it
   292	  conflicted with ENC's codec selection for a codec-known but unselected id.*
   293	
   294	### 3.4 Output
   295	
   296	Every report carries these six fields, and they are the stable set that later verifiers must reproduce: the verdict,
   297	its reason code, T, `revocation`, `historical_security` and standing. [A9:162; A9:268-283]
   298	
   299	- `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`. [A9:255-258]
   300	- `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply: an authenticated
   301	  safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared with T and A2.1's chain-time uncertainty. [A9:259-265]
   302	- Standing: `ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`, reported separately and never folded in. [A9:172-173; SPEC R-1.16:163]
   303	- No aggregate checkmark. [SPEC R-5.47:1419]
   304	- **Report encoding.** The stable-output object is the JCS object `{"historical_security":"NOT_EVALUATED",
   305	  "reason","revocation":"NOT_EVALUATED","standing":"ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE","T","verdict"}`; the success
   306	  reason is `OK`. T is the RFC 3339 string of nTime(h*) when the receipt proof parses, its file digest equals SHA256
   307	  of the presented `a_sig` bytes, no attestation in it is contradicted, and it has a qualifying attestation;
   308	  otherwise `null`. T is reported whatever the verdict. Diagnostics (trust-config version and hash, spec hash,
   309	  per-check list) go in a separate wrapper object, outside the stable six. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G32]
   310	- **UTC assessment** (outside the stable field set) [OWNER 2026-10-19, A10.1.1; A10:300-305; A10 row 22]. Every
   311	  receipt-verdict report, including a D1–D4 terminal report, contains the object `utc_assessment` =
   312	  `{"assumption_id":"A-UTC-7200-6","utc_upper_bound":U}`, defined in section 7.4. For D1–D4 its `utc_upper_bound`
   313	  is null. Startup refusals produce no receipt-verdict report. [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-03] *(Was: "Every report
   314	  reached after dispatch also carries the object …", which left D1–D4 reports undecided.)* It is never a member of the
   315	  stable-output object, never one of the six stable fields, never enters the verdict, the reason code or the
   316	  section 15.7 check order, and later verifiers are not bound to reproduce it as a stable field. It is reported
   317	  with every verdict, `INVALID` and `UNVERIFIABLE` included; a value reported with `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` does
   318	  not establish a valid service receipt or valid service signatures (A10:303-304). Documentation and relying-party
   319	  text never state U without the A-UTC identifier and the assumption beside it (A10:304-305).
   320	- **T for dispatch-terminal outcomes.** A D1–D4 outcome (section 3.1) evaluates nothing, the receipt proof
   321	  included, so its T is `null`; this holds for D4 too, even when the same bundle bytes under a strict request would
   322	  give a T. "T is reported whatever the verdict" applies to every outcome reached after dispatch.
   323	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-05] Its `utc_upper_bound` is likewise `null` (section 7.4).
   324	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, A10.1.1]
   325	- Relying-party text must say that a U reported with `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` is not evidence of a valid service
   326	  receipt, and must state A-UTC beside every U. [OWNER 2026-10-19, A10.1.1; A10:303-305]
   327	- Relying-party text must say that a T reported with `INVALID` is not evidence of a valid service receipt.
   328	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G32]
   329	- Relying-party text must say that `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can come from presenting a later proof, and that the
   330	  holder should be asked for the issuer's original proof. [A9:425-426]
   331	- Relying-party text must say that the verifier cannot check public publication of the git commit. [A9:495-500]
