   333	### 3.5 Verifier CLI and release-provisioning interface (instrument level)
   334	
   335	**Interface convention, not a verdict rule.** This subsection records how the conformance suite and the
   336	differential harness drive a verifier under test. It changes no verdict, reason or T, binds no production release,
   337	and gives way to the release artefact and release-signature format when those are decided (section 14.2 items 1,
   338	2). Both implementations of differential run 1 used it. [OWNER 2026-10-14, interface convention from diff-1,
   339	EA-01/EA-02/EA-03/EA-12] *The release format is now section 15.9. The TEST provisioning below is an instrument
   340	interface only: it is never a release, and an installed production verifier refuses it (section 15.9 R4).*
   341	[OWNER 2026-10-19, §14.2 item 2, `docs/V1-FREEZE-OPEN.md` §3]
   342	- **Invocation and compiled-in inputs (EA-01).** `CMD --trust <config> --bundle <bundle>`. The compiled-in trust
   343	  configuration hash and catalog (section 1, section 5) are provisioned for TEST by environment: the TEST release
   344	  record from `TESSERA_V1_TEST_RELEASE`, authenticated by the public key in `TESSERA_V1_TEST_RELEASE_KEY`, and the
   345	  catalog from `TESSERA_V1_TEST_CATALOG`. A config whose hash is not the one compiled by an authentically signed
   346	  release is refused (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict).
   347	- **Request (EA-02).** The request (D4) is `strict` by default; `--request <value>` supplies any other value.
   348	- **Output and refusal (EA-03).** A verdict is one JSON object on stdout carrying the six stable fields, either at
   349	  top level or under `stable`, `result` or `report`; diagnostics may accompany it. *Instrument convention
   350	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, A10.1.1]:* the independent verifier prints `{"stable":<stable-output object>,
   351	  "utc_assessment":<section 7.4 object>}`; the conformance runner compares `utc_assessment` only where a case's
   352	  `expected.json` carries one. A startup refusal is a nonzero
   353	  exit with the refusal code on stdout or stderr and no six-field object.
   354	- **Release checker (EA-12).** `CMD --previous <config> --next <config>` prints `ACCEPT` or `REJECT` for the release
   355	  invariants of section 5.
   356	
   357	## 4. Issue order (issuer side, binding on the build)
   358	
   359	1. Fix the manifest and common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   360	2. Build both frames.
   361	3. Sign each frame.
   362	4. Verify both signatures with the exact bytes to be committed.
   363	5. Freeze the signature bytes. Any re-framing happens before this point.
   364	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp its SHA-256. One stamp per receipt.
   365	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   366	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   367	
   368	[ENC §5.4:515-525; A9:293-296] That is eight steps in both sources. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a
   369	fresh stamp. The frame-only anchor is withdrawn, with no fallback. [A9:292; ENC §5.1:407-414]
   370	
   371	Issuer duties that are not verifier rules: an attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped [A9:422-423]; the
   372	`gpg` signer refuses a frame whose `declared_issue_time` is more than ε from its clock [A9:401-405]; the issuer never
   373	re-authorizes a key [A9:266]; public publication of the commit stays an issuer duty [A9:495-500]; the issuer
   374	re-captures the DNSSEC snapshot before shipping if it fails the snapshot rule [DD:94].
   375	
   376	Further issuer duties [OWNER 2026-10-14]:
   377	- **Chain-order pre-commitment.** Do not issue in a window until the key-assertion commit's stamp has k = 6
   378	  confirmations and the receipt will anchor at a higher height (A9 C4). Before honest issuance the issuer obtains and
   379	  validates the git assertion's k = 6 proof. A receipt stamped after the issuer has observed h_git + 5 anchors above
   380	  it, so it passes the verifier's `h_git + 5 < h_receipt` rule (section 6.3). [G02; RB §5]
   381	- **Snapshot c3.** Check all three conjuncts on the snapshot's own proof before shipping; recapture or retry if they
   382	  fail. A snapshot stamped before the receipt need not confirm before it. [G06]
   383	- **DNSSEC support.** Validate the snapshot under this profile's algorithm set (section 6.1); do not ship a receipt
   384	  whose chain would be `DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED`. [G16]
   385	- **Snapshot body.** Convert to the section 15.3 body before stamping. [G19]
   386	- **Signature output.** Both OpenPGP adapters emit signatures that satisfy the rule-O policy of section 8 before
   387	  signing is final and before stamping. [G11]
   388	- **Canonical container.** Emit the container as canonical JCS (section 15.1). [G17]
   389	- **Pre-ship checks for the 2026-10-14 r2 completions** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07, r2-F08, r2-F09, r2-F10]: the
   390	  snapshot body carries no RRSIG with fewer Labels than its owner (section 15.3); the selected path of each of the
   391	  three proofs (receipt, snapshot, git) satisfies the transaction-shape subset and the conventional merkle suffix
   392	  (section 15.2), else the proof is re-stamped or the attempt abandoned; both OpenPGP adapters and the repository
   393	  signer emit type-16/type-33 subpackets only in the shapes section 8 allows. All 352 Bitcoin paths in the
   394	  repository's 129 proofs satisfy the shape and suffix rules (owner check, 2026-10-14); this is evidence, not a rule.
   395	
   396	## 5. Trust configuration
   397	
   398	It ships with the verifier and is never read from the bundle. [A9:154; SPEC R-5.8:1217; SPEC R-2.17:262] It holds:
   399	
   400	| # | Item | Source |
   401	|---|---|---|
   402	| 1 | Root KSK-2017 | [A9:154-155; DD:66] |
   403	| 2 | Root KSK-2024 | [A9:154-155; DD:66] |
   404	| 3 | Repository entries: D = `wamason.com`, the repository key, an inclusive signing interval | OWNER PROFILE DECISION 2026-10-13 (section 6.3); A9.5 repository entries; [A9:68-69] |
   405	| 4 | A versioned set of pinned mainnet header hashes, authenticated by the release allowlist | [A9:155; DD:66]; A9.5 trust-configuration authority |
   406	| 5 | ~~The parameters δ, ε, k~~ | moved to the frozen spec catalog (section 1) [OWNER 2026-10-14, G12] |
   407	
   408	*Dated correction 2026-10-14:* item 5 previously read "The parameters δ, ε, k [A9:217; SPEC R-5.8:1217]". A
   409	trust-configuration release can be extended, so verdict-deciding parameters now live in the frozen spec catalog,
   410	which cannot. Item 3 previously read "The pair (D, repository root key)"; A9.5's repository entries also carry a
   411	signing interval.
   412	
   413	- **Authority: independent release allowlisting** (A9.5 "Trust-configuration authority", OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14).
   414	  The trust configuration is authenticated independently of the bundle and of both authority-channel credentials.
   415	  Each verifier release compiles in the SHA-256 of exactly one trust configuration; the config file itself carries no
   416	  signature. Releases are signed by the **release key**, which neither the DNS credential, the repository key nor the
   417	  issuing AWS account can use; the dated custody record names that key and its access boundary. Capture of any
   418	  authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release, authorize a configuration hash, or replace the
   419	  compiled trust root or policy catalog. CI is not the authenticity check. Compromise of the release key is
   420	  distribution compromise, a stated boundary (A9 "The boundaries"). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G08]
   421	  - A config whose SHA-256 is not the compiled hash, or that fails the section 15.5 schema, makes the verifier refuse
   422	    to run with exit error `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`: no verdict.
   423	  - Bootstrap: the production release key's full v4 fingerprint is published through at least two channels
   424	    independent of the operational environment and recorded in the dated first-production-release bootstrap record
   425	    (section 15.9 R5; A10.4.2). The freeze record does not contain it. Initial bootstrap authenticates the first
   426	    production key. A successor is authenticated by a conforming release verified under the currently authenticated
   427	    key and naming the successor fingerprint; subsequently acquired successor key material must match that
   428	    fingerprint. Loss recovery starts a separately authenticated release lineage and does not establish continuity
   429	    with the old lineage (section 15.9 R5). [OWNER 2026-10-19, §14.2 item 2; A10.4.2 "Bootstrap record"; OWNER
   430	    2026-10-19, PR-05] *(Was, 2026-10-19: "A new release key is introduced only by a release signed by the current
   431	    one", which recovery contradicted.)* *(Was: "the
   432	    release public key is published with the verifier's documentation and relying-party text, and its fingerprint is
   433	    in the freeze record.")*
   434	  - `version` starts at `"1"` and increases by 1; `previous_sha256` is 64 zeros for version 1, else the prior config's
   435	    hash. Every report carries the config version and hash as diagnostics; reproducing a verdict exactly (A9.4) uses
   436	    the release that compiled that hash.
   437	  - Required test: a captured repository signer (and, separately, a captured DNS credential and the issuing AWS
   438	    account) signs a config adding its own KSK, repository key and pins; the verifier refuses it.
   439	  - **OPEN:** release-key custody, holder and access boundary (section 14.2 item 1; gate: the first public verifier
   440	    release, A10.4.2). The release artefact and release-signature format are section 15.9. [OWNER 2026-10-19,
   441	    §14.2 item 2]
   442	  - *Dated correction 2026-10-14:* the 2026-10-13 resolutions draft proposed a dedicated trust-config key giving the
   443	    config a detached rule-O signature, with append-only enforced in CI. Withdrawn: key inequality does not establish
   444	    independence, and CI cannot stop an attacker who bypasses it (`CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md` F04).
   445	
   446	- **Root KSKs, three periods each.** The trust configuration records each KSK's signing and reliance periods
   447	  separately from its publication and revocation dates. [A9:154-155; A9:324-325] The instrument spike found that
   448	  conflating signing with publication falsely rejected every snapshot from 2026-10-11. [SR:73-78]
   449	- **Root rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time if some trusted KSK whose **reliance
   450	  period** contains that time validates it. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance lifetime L; the reliance
   451	  interval is [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is an acceptance-policy value from cited RRSIG validity evidence,
   452	  labelled observed, not a proven global maximum. The extension admits authentic pre-retirement evidence anchored
   453	  later and does not prove signing before retirement: a thief of a retired KSK can manufacture eligible evidence
