   631	## 6. Authority channels
   632	
   633	Both channels must pass (n = 2). Neither commits to any receipt. [A9:143, 153] Both carry the same issuance
   634	window. [A9:407-408; ENC §7.1:630-634]
   635	
   636	### 6.1 DNS channel
   637	
   638	- Owner name is `_tessera-v1.<D>`. The verifier rebuilds it from the signed `issuer` and compares label by label,
   639	  ignoring ASCII case. Anything else is `DNS_OWNER`. [ENC §7:551-556] This is the owner-name check (G-21 / M2).
   640	- One TXT RRset, one RR, one character-string. The value grammar, the fifteen-step rejection order and the
   641	  exact-255-byte rule are in ENC §7. [ENC §7:557-612]
   642	- The record carries `kms`, `gpg`, `profile`, `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after`, all mandatory, in a
   643	  fixed order. [ENC §7:569-579]
   644	- The DNSSEC chain is validated against the root rule (section 5) at the snapshot's own anchored time (section 7.3).
   645	  [A9:143; A9:309-310]
   646	- **Root rule first.** The root RRset passes only under A9.5: a byte-matching configured KSK, usable in its reliance
   647	  interval at T_snap, verifies a selected RRSIG valid at T_snap. The algorithm profile below never replaces that
   648	  eligibility condition. Candidate aggregation is existential and order-independent; extra candidates are
   649	  informational after any eligible supported path passes. Root outcomes and their precedence are section 5's.
   650	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G16]
   651	- **Supported DNSSEC set (NARROW-CLAIM, visible owner scope choice).** DNSKEY and RRSIG algorithms 8 (RSA/SHA-256,
   652	  RFC 5702, key per RFC 3110) and 13 (ECDSA P-256/SHA-256, RFC 6605); DS digest types 2 (SHA-256, RFC 4509) and 4
   653	  (SHA-384, RFC 6605). The DNSKEY used has the Zone Key flag set, REVOKE clear, protocol 3. Validation is RFC 4035
   654	  §5.3.1–5.3.3 at T_snap with RFC 4034 §6 canonical form. DS is signed by a key in the parent's validated DNSKEY
   655	  set; a child DNSKEY RRset is self-signed by a key matching a validated DS; the TXT is signed by a key in its zone's
   656	  validated DNSKEY set. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G16]
   657	- **Outcomes for each non-root required RRset**, computed once after every candidate is tried [OWNER 2026-10-14,
   658	  G16]:
   659	  1. some supported path (matching authenticated DS → DNSKEY → RRSIG) validates at T_snap: pass;
   660	  2. else the RRset or the witness RRSIG it needs is missing: `UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`;
   661	  3. else an unsupported algorithm or digest type could supply the path: `UNVERIFIABLE /
   662	     DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED`;
   663	  4. else (every possibility supported and checked) a candidate verifies but its window excludes T_snap, or its
   664	     times are edge failures (section 7.3): `INVALID / RRSIG_NOT_VALID_AT_EVAL_TIME` (or that edge reason);
   665	  5. else a supported candidate fails cryptographically: `INVALID / DNSSEC_SIGNATURE_INVALID`;
   666	  6. else no child DNSKEY matches a validated DS: `INVALID / DNSKEY_DS_MISMATCH`.
   667	- **Complete candidate table** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F05]. Supersedes the six rows above where they differ. DS →
   668	  DNSKEY matching (DNSKEY RRsets) is separate from RRset → RRSIG → DNSKEY matching (DS and TXT RRsets, and each
   669	  DNSKEY RRset's self-signature). Each candidate takes the first failing RFC 4035 §5.3.1 precondition below; the
   670	  RRset outcome is computed once, after every candidate:
   671	  1. some supported eligible path validates at T_snap: pass;
   672	  2. else the RRset, its witness RRSIG, or the signer zone's DNSKEY RRset is absent: `UNVERIFIABLE /
   673	     DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`;
   674	  3. else a **potentially usable unsupported path** exists: `UNVERIFIABLE / DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED`. Defined
   675	     by authenticated DS metadata and matching selectors only: for a DNSKEY RRset, an authenticated DS of
   676	     unsupported digest type or algorithm whose key tag and algorithm match a child DNSKEY; for any RRset, an RRSIG
   677	     of unsupported algorithm whose signer name, algorithm and key tag match a DNSKEY in the signer zone's
   678	     authenticated set. An unrelated unsupported RR does not qualify;
   679	  4. else the parent's authentication is blocked (the parent RRset's outcome is unperformable) and some candidate is
   680	     locally valid (well-formed, metadata consistent, verifies under a supplied matching DNSKEY of its signer zone,
   681	     window contains T_snap): the authenticated-path check is unperformable and carries no reason of its own (the
   682	     parent's reason is earlier in section 15.7);
   683	  5. else, `INVALID`, by the most-progressed failing candidate, in this order: verifies but window or edge failure
   684	     (`RRSIG_NOT_VALID_AT_EVAL_TIME`, `RRSIG_TIME_AMBIGUOUS`, `RRSIG_INTERVAL_REVERSED`); supported candidate fails
   685	     cryptographically (`DNSSEC_SIGNATURE_INVALID`); present supported witness with no matching eligible DNSKEY
   686	     (signer name, algorithm and key tag matching a DNSKEY with Zone Key set, protocol 3, REVOKE clear) in the
   687	     signer zone's set (`DNSSEC_KEY_MISMATCH`); no child DNSKEY matches a validated DS (`DNSKEY_DS_MISMATCH`);
   688	     Labels greater than the owner's label count, or other non-temporal metadata mismatch (`DNSSEC_RRSIG_METADATA`);
   689	     malformed supported DNSKEY or RRSIG RDATA (`DNSSEC_FORMAT`). Ties within a class: G35 candidate order.
   690	  Locally obtainable checks (framing, TXT grammar, signature mathematics against supplied keys) run whether or not
   691	  the parent is authenticated; under row 4 they become reportable failures (row 5) only when no candidate of that
   692	  RRset is locally valid, which preserves the existential rule. This is the bridge from RFC 4035's failures to the
   693	  V1 partition, not a claim that RFC 4035 is unspecified. **Owner choice (fail-closed):** an unmatched selector is
   694	  `INVALID / DNSSEC_KEY_MISMATCH`, not insufficient evidence; the honest issuer validates the chain before
   695	  shipping. *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: the six rows above gave no outcome for an unmatched key tag, Labels
   696	  defects, bad flags/protocol or malformed supported RDATA, and "an unsupported algorithm could supply the path" did
   697	  not define which candidates count.)*
   698	  - **Unrelated unsupported witness only.** An RRSIG of an unsupported algorithm whose signer name, algorithm and
   699	    key tag match no DNSKEY in the signer zone's set is neither a potentially usable unsupported path (row 3) nor a
   700	    supported witness (row 5's `DNSSEC_KEY_MISMATCH`). If it is the only RRSIG covering the RRset, the RRset has no
   701	    usable witness: `UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING` (row 2). The fail-closed unmatched-selector choice above
   702	    applies to supported-algorithm witnesses only. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-11]
   703	  - **RSA signature width.** An algorithm-8 RRSIG whose signature field is not exactly the modulus length of the
   704	    DNSKEY it is checked against is malformed supported RRSIG RDATA, `INVALID / DNSSEC_FORMAT`, not
   705	    `DNSSEC_SIGNATURE_INVALID`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, A13]
   706	- **Relying-party text (visible limit).** A chain that moves to an algorithm outside {8, 13} (for example Ed25519,
   707	  algorithm 15) makes new snapshots `UNVERIFIABLE` under this frozen spec; the issuer does not ship such receipts.
   708	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G16]
   709	- **No wildcards (NARROW-CLAIM).** A TXT RRSIG whose Labels field is less than the owner's label count gives
   710	  `INVALID / DNS_WILDCARD`. The issuer publishes an explicit record. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G19]
   711	  - **Owner choice: a global body restriction, not witness eligibility** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07]. The rule
   712	    above is a **transport restriction on the snapshot body** (the "no-wildcard body restriction"), separate from
   713	    witness eligibility: any RRSIG anywhere in the body whose Labels field is less than its owner's label count
   714	    gives `INVALID / DNS_WILDCARD`, even beside a valid explicit-owner witness. Accordingly the promise that extra
   715	    candidates are informational after an eligible path passes (above, and section 7.3) does **not** cover such an
   716	    RRSIG. Chosen as the fail-closed reading; the honest issuer publishes an explicit record and checks the body
   717	    before stamping (section 4). *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: the bullet left open whether an extra wildcard
   718	    candidate beside a valid explicit witness rejects the snapshot; it now does. It previously named only TXT
   719	    RRSIGs.)*
   720	- **Inherited residual.** An ancestor zone (root, `com`) can sign data for names below it; V1 inherits that DNSSEC
   721	  trust, and the git channel (n = 2) guards against it. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G19]
