   908	### 7.2 Header pins
   909	
   910	- Every bundle header must be in the pinned set. Otherwise the verdict is `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`.
   911	  There is no retarget logic, because the pins cover every accepted header. [A9:156; DD:99]
   912	- **Conflict.** A supplied header at a pinned height with a different hash gives `INVALID / HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN`:
   913	  it is contradictory evidence. A9.1's "a bundle header outside the pins" is read as a header **at a height the pins
   914	  do not cover**. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G23] A10.5 stage 1 (signed 2026-10-18) registers this reading as A9's meaning:
   915	  no pin at the height gives `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`, a header differing from its pin gives `INVALID /
   916	  HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN`. [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura contested 2] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict for a header that … conflicts with a
   917	  pin", 2026-10-13.)*
   918	- **Coverage.** Required: headers h* … h*+5 of the selected attestation of each of the three proofs (receipt,
   919	  snapshot, git), all in the shared `headers` pool. Every supplied header, used or not, is checked against the pins.
   920	  A `headers` entry that fails row 2.4 is not a supplied header: it contributes `CONTAINER_SCHEMA` only, and rows
   921	  10.x, 11.x, 13.4 and 14.6 use the remaining entries [OWNER 2026-10-14, diff-1 F-3].
   922	  For each pair of supplied headers at consecutive heights, a prev-hash mismatch gives `INVALID /
   923	  HEADER_CHAIN_BROKEN`; a missing intermediate height is not a broken link. No PoW, nBits or retarget check: a hash
   924	  equal to a pin implies all of it. At an unselected height, a pinned header with missing successors is ignored,
   925	  while an unpinned header blocks the bundle (`HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G07, G05] *(Was: "`OPEN
   926	  (step 8):` which heights the pins must cover for a given proof", 2026-10-13.)*
   927	
   928	### 7.3 The DNSSEC snapshot rule
   929	
   930	- The issuer anchors the DNSSEC snapshot with its own OTS proof. The envelope is ENC §6 under the tag
   931	  `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot`. [DD:94; ENC §6:536-542]
   932	- The verifier checks that the snapshot's anchored time lies inside every RRSIG window in the chain and within
   933	  [declared − ε, declared + δ]. The issuer re-captures before shipping if it does not. [DD:94] This is an owner
   934	  decision carried in, not routed. [A9:104-106]
   935	  - "declared" is the receipt's signed `declared_issue_time`, not the snapshot's capture time. Capture-to-anchor
   936	    latency is a separate observation, not this predicate. (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F6, accepted 2026-10-13.)
   937	  - "every RRSIG window in the chain" means the one witness signature selected to authenticate each required RRset,
   938	    not every signature offered: an extra expired or invalid signature beside a valid one neither fails the RRset
   939	    nor narrows the joint window. The instrument's selection order is (key tag, inception, expiration, signature
   940	    bytes), first that validates. (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F5, accepted 2026-10-13.) Exception: an RRSIG
   941	    with fewer Labels than its owner fails the body restriction wherever it appears (section 6.1).
   942	    [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07]
   943	  - **Frozen witness rule.** The selected witness is the **first eligible validating** candidate in (key tag,
   944	    inception, expiration, signature bytes, canonical RRSIG RDATA, canonical key RDATA) order. For the root, reliance
   945	    eligibility precedes selection. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G35] *(Previously described only as the instrument's order.)*
   946	  - Conjunct 3 (`confirmed_at` of the snapshot ≤ declared + δ) is a new owner acceptance restriction, fail-closed. A
   947	    failure of any conjunct gives `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_TIME`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G06]
   948	- The chain is evaluated at the snapshot's anchored time, not at T of the receipt. [A9:143]
   949	- Why: the joint RRSIG window measured 1.6 days, shorter than δ (M8). [DD:93]
   950	- **Snapshot body.** A fixed-order sequence of uncompressed RFC 1035 wire RRs in RFC 4034 §6.2 canonical form,
   951	  section 15.3. Framing violations are `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`; a syntactically complete body lacking a required
   952	  RRset or its witness RRSIG is `UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING` (root: `NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE`). The conversion
   953	  from resolver messages happens before stamping and changes S, so each snapshot needs a fresh stamp; earlier
   954	  stamped JSON snapshots cannot be reused. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G19] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the snapshot body
   955	  format", 2026-10-13.)*
   956	- **Snapshot time.** T_snap = T of the snapshot's own proof under the section 7.1 rule, at the same depth k = 6, and
   957	  `confirmed_at`(snapshot) likewise. DNSSEC is evaluated at T_snap only; no capture time, receipt T or wall clock is
   958	  ever used. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G06] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` how the snapshot's anchored time is derived",
   959	  2026-10-13.)*
   960	- **RRSIG time arithmetic.** Inception and expiration are 32-bit values v, each resolved to the unique absolute
   961	  second t with t ≡ v (mod 2^32) and |t − T_snap| < 2^31 (RFC 4034 §3.1.5, RFC 1982). If v − T_snap ≡ 2^31 (mod 2^32)
   962	  the candidate fails with `RRSIG_TIME_AMBIGUOUS`; if resolved inception > expiration it fails with
   963	  `RRSIG_INTERVAL_REVERSED`; inception = expiration is allowed. These are candidate failures under the existential
   964	  rule, ranked with `RRSIG_NOT_VALID_AT_EVAL_TIME`. The witness check is inception ≤ T_snap ≤ expiration, never "now",
   965	  including in 2106. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G27]
