  1501	## 15. Evidence formats and check order [OWNER 2026-10-14]
  1502	
  1503	### 15.1 Container [OWNER 2026-10-14, G14, G17, G33]
  1504	
  1505	A bundle is one UTF-8 JSON text that passes ENC §1.4 checks 1–5 and 7 and is canonical JCS. It contains no JSON
  1506	numbers. Heights and durations are decimal strings matching `0|[1-9][0-9]*` with value at most 4294967295. Binary
  1507	values are RFC 4648 §4 base64 with padding that round-trips (no non-zero pad bits, no line breaks). Size cap 2^27
  1508	bytes. Top level: exactly `{"layers":[L],"profile":"tessera-v1-strict"}`. After dispatch (section 3.1): a
  1509	non-canonical container is `INVALID / JSON_NONCANONICAL`; then the absence rule (section 2.1) and A9.5's
  1510	`git.object_format` outcomes; then any other schema failure is `INVALID / CONTAINER_SCHEMA`. Absence is inspected
  1511	only beneath correctly typed parents, a present non-string `object_format` is `CONTAINER_SCHEMA`, and a present
  1512	`issuer_record` with an absent internal field is `CONTAINER_SCHEMA` (section 2.1). [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F03]
  1513	
  1514	**Numbers.** JSON numbers are admitted syntactically at dispatch (D1's strict-JSON checks do not exclude them, and
  1515	D2/D3 read only `profile` and the length of `layers`) and rejected after dispatch by the container schema: a JSON
  1516	number anywhere in the layer is `INVALID / CONTAINER_SCHEMA` (row 2.4, after row 2.1), never `OUT_OF_PROFILE`.
  1517	[OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, A10]
  1518	
  1519	Layer `L` (closed; each member exactly once; all values base64 unless marked):
  1520	
  1521	| Member | JSON type and content | Invariants |
  1522	|---|---|---|
  1523	| `a_sig` | string: `A_sig` bytes | decoded by ENC §5 |
  1524	| `manifest` | string: `F_manifest` | decoded by ENC §4 |
  1525	| `possession` | object `{"gpg","kms"}`: detached signature over `F_manifest` per slot rule | both present |
  1526	| `keys` | object `{"gpg","kms"}` (section 8) | both present |
  1527	| `receipt_anchor` | object `{"ots","txid"}`; `txid` 64 lowercase hex, display order | |
  1528	| `headers` | object `{"<height>":"<160 lowercase hex>"}` (80-byte raw header) | height grammar; nonempty; shared pool |
  1529	| `dns` | object `{"ots","snapshot_body"}` | body per 15.3 |
  1530	| `git` | object `{"blob","commit","object_format","ots","trees"}`; `trees` array of exactly 2 strings [root, sub]; `object_format` string | A9.5 |
  1531	| `spec`, `vectors`, `preamble`, `dependency_manifest` | string: raw bytes | hashed only |
  1532	| `issuer_record` | optional object `{"observed_anchor_delay_s","policy_version"}`; delay in the decimal grammar (seconds); `policy_version` matches `tessera-v1-issue-policy/(0|[1-9][0-9]*)` | diagnostics only |
  1533	
  1534	### 15.2 OTS proofs and the txid [OWNER 2026-10-14, G20]
  1535	
  1536	OWNER CHOICE (supported-path limit). python-opentimestamps 0.4.5 is the reference reader, not normative.
  1537	- **Header.** Magic `00 4f70656e54696d657374616d7073 0000 50726f6f66 00 bf89e2e884e89294`, varuint major version 1,
  1538	  file-hash op `08` (SHA-256 only), the 32-byte digest, one Timestamp, end of input.
  1539	- **Timestamp.** `Timestamp := (ff Item)* Item`; `Item := 00 Attestation | Op Timestamp`.
  1540	- **Ops.** `08` (sha256), `f0 varbytes` (append), `f1 varbytes` (prepend). Arguments 1–4096 bytes; every message and
  1541	  result at most 4096 bytes.
  1542	- **Attestations.** `tag(8) varbytes(payload)`, payload at most 8192 bytes. Bitcoin `0588960d73d71901`: payload is
  1543	  exactly one varuint height. Pending `83dfe30d2ef90c8e`: payload varbytes(URI ≤ 1000), URI ignored.
  1544	- **Wire completion** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F09]. `varbytes := varuint byte_count || exactly byte_count octets`.
  1545	  Both the pending and the Bitcoin payloads must be consumed exactly (the pending payload's inner URI framing
  1546	  included); otherwise `OTS_FORMAT`. A Bitcoin attestation whose message is not exactly 32 bytes is `INVALID /
  1547	  OTS_FORMAT`, independently of header availability. Timestamp depth: the root Timestamp has depth 0, each Op →
  1548	  Timestamp step adds 1, `ff` siblings do not add. Op and attestation counts are of serialized items, duplicates
  1549	  included; duplicates are semantically inert only within those limits.
  1550	- **Varuint.** LEB128, shortest form only, value ≤ 2^32 − 1. (python-opentimestamps 0.4.5 accepts redundant groups;
  1551	  V1 does not.)
  1552	- **Bounds.** Depth ≤ 256; ≤ 65536 ops and ≤ 4096 attestations per proof; proof ≤ 2^20 bytes. Duplicate identical
  1553	  branches are permitted and have no effect.
  1554	- **Rejections.** Any other op or tag, or any grammar or bound violation: `INVALID / OTS_FORMAT`. File digest must be
  1555	  SHA256(`A_sig`) for the receipt (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`), SHA256 of the ENC §6 envelope for the snapshot
  1556	  (`SNAPSHOT_STAMP_MISMATCH`), SHA256 of `<40-hex commit id>\n` for git (`GIT_STAMP_MISMATCH`).
  1557	- **Merkle link.** A Bitcoin attestation's message is 32 bytes and equals bytes 36–68 of the bundled header at that
  1558	  height.
  1559	- **Header fields** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F09]. In a raw 80-byte header: prev = `header[4:36]`, merkle root =
  1560	  `header[36:68]`, nTime = `uint32le(header[68:72])`.
  1561	- **txid (supported-proof subset).** On the path to the **selected** attestation, let the first two consecutive
  1562	  sha256 ops have input P, and let m be the message immediately before the maximal run of append/prepend ops that
  1563	  precedes them. Required: (1) P parses exactly, consuming every byte, as a non-witness Bitcoin transaction —
  1564	  version (4), compact-size input count ≥ 1, inputs (32 + 4 + compact-size script length + script + 4), compact-size
  1565	  output count ≥ 1, outputs (8 + compact-size length + script), locktime (4); no SegWit marker; compact sizes
  1566	  shortest form; (2) m is 32 bytes and some output script is exactly `6a 20 ‖ m`; (3) `hex(reverse(SHA256d(P)))`
  1567	  equals `receipt_anchor.txid`. Otherwise `INVALID / ANCHOR_TXID_MISMATCH`. Any one path to the selected height may
  1568	  satisfy this. (All 344 Bitcoin paths in the repository's 127 proofs had this shape when RES G20 was written; the
  1569	later owner check of 2026-10-14 under part (4) below counts 352 paths in 129 proofs, all conforming. Evidence, not a
  1570	rule. [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura contested 6])
  1571	- **CompactSize and transaction fields** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F09]. A first byte < 253 is the value; `fd` then u16le,
  1572	  `fe` then u32le, `ff` then u64le; shortest form only; a length or count exceeding the remaining bytes is rejected
  1573	  before allocation. Version, value and locktime are parsed as their fixed-width bytes (4, 8, 4) and not
  1574	  interpreted. Consensus validity and script execution are supplied by authentic pinned history, not revalidated
  1575	  here.
  1576	- **(4) Merkle suffix (owner choice, fail-closed)** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F09]. After the two sha256 ops that
  1577	  produce the txid, the path to the selected attestation consists only of zero or more repetitions of (append or
  1578	  prepend of exactly 32 bytes, sha256, sha256); an empty suffix is a one-transaction block. Otherwise `INVALID /
  1579	  ANCHOR_TXID_MISMATCH` for the receipt. Without it, arbitrary allowed operations could follow the identified
  1580	  transaction. All 352 Bitcoin paths in the repository's 129 proofs have this suffix (owner check 2026-10-14;
  1581	  evidence, not a rule). This is a missing structural definition, not a demonstrated forged timestamp.
  1582	- **Scope** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F08]. Parts (1), (2) and (4) apply at the selected height of the receipt, snapshot
  1583	  and git proofs; part (3), the txid equality, applies to `receipt_anchor` only. Snapshot or git failure of (1), (2)
  1584	  or (4) at every qualifying path at its h*: `INVALID / OTS_TX_SHAPE`. h* is selected first (section 7.1); a failing
  1585	  path never moves selection to a higher height.
  1586	
  1587	### 15.3 DNSSEC snapshot body [OWNER 2026-10-14, G19, G24, G30]
  1588	
  1589	A concatenation of RRs, each in RFC 1035 §3.2.1 wire format, uncompressed, in RFC 4034 §6.2 canonical form (owner
  1590	name and every domain-name field inside RDATA, including the RRSIG signer name, lowercase and uncompressed).
  1591	1. **Groups, in order:** the root DNSKEY group; then for each zone z below the root down to the TXT's signer zone, a
  1592	   DS group at z and a DNSKEY group at z; last the TXT group at `_tessera-v1.<D>`. A group is a maximal run of RRs
  1593	   with the same owner, CLASS IN, and TYPE t (the RRset) or TYPE RRSIG with Type Covered t (its witnesses). The RRset
  1594	   comes first, in RFC 4034 §6.3 canonical RDATA order without duplicates, then zero or more RRSIGs in (key tag,
  1595	   inception, expiration, signature bytes) order. Each z is a proper descendant of the previous zone. Signer names:
  1596	   the parent for DS, z for DNSKEY, the root for the root group, the last z for TXT.
  1597	   - **Serialization ties and duplicates** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07]. Canonical RRSIG RDATA is the final body
  1598	     serialization sort key: RRSIGs sort by (key tag, inception, expiration, signature bytes, canonical RRSIG
  1599	     RDATA). A duplicate is an RR identical in owner, class, type and RDATA (`SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`); TTL consistency
  1600	     within an RRset is checked separately (point 2).
  1601	   - **Raw time fields in sort keys.** Inception and expiration in these sort keys, and in the G35 candidate tuple
  1602	     (section 7.3), are the raw 32-bit unsigned RRSIG field values compared numerically (equivalently, as
  1603	     big-endian bytes), not the absolute times resolved by section 7.3's serial arithmetic; the two orders differ
  1604	     only across the 2^32 wrap. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-13]
  1605	   - **Too-short RRSIG.** An RRSIG whose RDATA (within a consistent RDLENGTH) is too short for RFC 4034 §3.1's
  1606	     fixed fields and signer name is attributed to the group of the immediately preceding RRs at the same owner,
  1607	     not treated as a framing failure, and is malformed supported RRSIG RDATA: `INVALID / DNSSEC_FORMAT`
  1608	     (section 6.1). [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, A06]
  1609	   - **Chain skeleton, recovery grammar** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F06]. The root is the initial zone. The ordered zone
  1610	     skeleton is constructed from the supplied DS and DNSKEY owners and the RRSIG signer names: each names a zone,
  1611	     and the zones, ordered by descent, must form one chain from the root. Omitted DS, DNSKEY, root DNSKEY or TXT
  1612	     groups are admitted as missing evidence (`UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`; root:
  1613	     `NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE`); omission alone is never a format failure. Direct signing of the TXT by the root, or
  1614	     by any ancestor zone in the skeleton, is permitted (section 6.1 retains ancestor signing authority); "the last
  1615	     z" is the TXT RRSIG's signer zone, which is the root when no below-root zone is supplied. Conflicting supplied
  1616	     transitions or signer names (names not forming one descent chain, a DS group not signed by its parent zone in
  1617	     the skeleton, a DNSKEY group not signed by its own zone, a group after the TXT group) are `INVALID /
  1618	     SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`. A supplied final TXT owner is checked by ENC §7's `DNS_OWNER`, even when wrong; its existence
  1619	     does not itself constitute an "outside-chain" framing failure. *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: the grammar did
  1620	     not say whether a skipped zone was omission or inconsistency, and "the last z" was undefined for a TXT signed
  1621	     by the root.)*
  1622	2. **Fields.** CLASS IN. TTL equal within an RRset and otherwise uninterpreted; validation uses the RRSIG's Original
  1623	   TTL.
  1624	3. **Framing versus completeness.** A truncated RR encoding, forbidden RR type, duplicate RR, inconsistent
  1625	   owner/type within a group, or misordered supplied group is `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`. Among RDATA defects,
  1626	   RR boundary/RDLENGTH truncation and any domain-name field inside RDATA that is not in the canonical form above (an
  1627	   uppercase ASCII letter or a compression pointer, including in the RRSIG signer name) are `INVALID /
  1628	   SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`, whatever other witnesses the body carries; TXT's internal character-string parsing uses ENC's
  1629	   `DNS_RDATA` / `DNS_STRINGS`, and other malformed supported DNSKEY/RRSIG RDATA is `DNSSEC_FORMAT` (section 6.1).
  1630	   [OWNER 2026-10-14, diff-1 F-1: the earlier wording let a non-canonical RRSIG beside a valid witness reach
  1631	   `VALID_STRICT` in one implementation and `INVALID` in another; fail-closed chosen.]
  1632	   A defect in an RR's owner-name wire encoding is `SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`; a defect of the RRSIG signer-name field
  1633	   other than the non-canonical forms just named (which are `SNAPSHOT_FORMAT` under diff-1 F-1) and other than a
  1634	   recovery-grammar conflict is malformed supported RRSIG RDATA, `DNSSEC_FORMAT`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from
  1635	   diff-1, A12]
  1636	   [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F06, r2-F05] A syntactically complete
  1637	   sequence lacking a required RRset or its witness RRSIG is `UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING` (root:
  1638	   `NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE`). Missing groups alone are not order violations. Forbidden: any type other than DNSKEY, DS,
  1639	   TXT and RRSIG, and any RR at a name outside the chain (the final TXT group excepted, see the recovery grammar);
  1640	   CNAME and DNAME are not followed. An empty body is `LENGTH_CAP`.
  1641	4. **Wildcards:** `INVALID / DNS_WILDCARD` (section 6.1). This is the global no-wildcard body restriction: any
  1642	   RRSIG in the body with Labels less than its owner's label count, whatever other witnesses the body carries.
  1643	   [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07] The Labels octet is read, and this restriction applied, whenever an RRSIG's RDATA
  1644	   has at least 4 bytes (Type Covered, Algorithm, Labels), however malformed the rest of that RDATA is.
  1645	   [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, A07]
  1646	5. **Envelope:** ENC §6, `header ‖ u32 len ‖ body`, 1 ≤ len ≤ 2^24.
  1647	
  1648	### 15.4 Git evidence [OWNER 2026-10-14, G25]
  1649	
  1650	OWNER CHOICE (supported-path limit) plus SHA-1DC (BROADEN).
  1651	- **Commit body** (without `commit <len>\0`): `tree <40 lhex>LF`, `(parent <40 lhex>LF)*`, `author <bytes>LF`,
  1652	  `committer <bytes>LF`, exactly one `gpgsig <line>LF` with `( <line>LF)*` continuations, `LF`, message. Repeated
  1653	  `parent` headers with distinct ids are allowed (merges); a repeated identical id, any other header (`encoding`,
  1654	  `mergetag`, `gpgsig-sha256`, unknown), or a second `tree`, `author`, `committer` or `gpgsig` gives
  1655	  `INVALID / GIT_COMMIT_FORMAT`. Author and committer are not interpreted. The signed payload is the commit without
  1656	  the `gpgsig` line and its continuations (gitformat-signature(5)).
  1657	  - **Empty identity values.** The `<bytes>` of `author` and of `committer` are nonempty: `author` or `committer`
  1658	    followed by an empty value is `INVALID / GIT_COMMIT_FORMAT`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, A09]
  1659	- **Signature value** (continuations joined by LF, one leading space stripped from each): `-----BEGIN PGP
  1660	  SIGNATURE-----` LF, one empty line, one or more base64 lines of 1–76 RFC 4648 characters (`=` padding only at the
  1661	  end of the last), an optional CRC line `=` plus 4 base64 characters, `-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----`, no trailing LF.
  1662	  No armor headers, no other blank lines, no whitespace inside lines. A present CRC-24 must match. Dearmored bytes
  1663	  satisfy rule O's grammar and policy (section 8) with type 0x00. Otherwise `INVALID / GIT_SIG_FORMAT`.
  1664	  The concatenated base64 lines obey strict RFC 4648 round-trip and padding (no nonzero pad bits), as in section
  1665	  15.1; CRC presence and validity remain as above. Every armor, codec and policy violation is `GIT_SIG_FORMAT`, the
  1666	  stated exception to section 8's `SIG_POLICY`; failed mathematics is `GIT_SIGNER_NOT_CONFIGURED` (section 6.3).
  1667	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F10, r2-F11]
  1668	- **Trees.** Entries `mode SP name NUL id20`; modes {40000, 100644, 100755, 120000, 160000} without leading zero;
  1669	  names nonempty, no `/` or NUL, not `.` or `..`; strictly sorted in git order (trees compare as `name/`), no
  1670	  duplicates, body consumed exactly. Otherwise `INVALID / GIT_TREE_FORMAT`.
  1671	- **Path.** Root entry `tessera-v1` (40000), then `key-assertion` (100644), the blob; `git.trees` is exactly
  1672	  [root, sub]. Otherwise `INVALID / GIT_PATH`.
  1673	  - A commit whose `tree` header names an id other than the SHA-1 id of `git.trees[0]` is `INVALID / GIT_PATH`
  1674	    (row 14.5); there is no dedicated code. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-22]
  1675	- **Ids.** SHA-1 of `"<type> <len>\0" ‖ body` with collision detection (SHA-1DC); a detection is
  1676	  `INVALID / GIT_SHA1_COLLISION`. SHA-1DC is a pinned detection implementation and version named in the dependency
  1677	  manifest, with its prescribed collision-return behaviour, not whatever a library calls SHA-1 (section 14.2 item
  1678	  15). [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F14]
