  1725	### 15.6 Dependency manifest [OWNER 2026-10-14, G15]
  1726	
  1727	A closed JCS object, exactly `{"entries":[…],"format":"tessera-v1-deps/1"}`; `entries` sorted by `id`, unique. Each
  1728	entry has all of: `id` (`[a-z0-9-]+`), `title`, `edition`, `sections` (nonempty array of strings), `errata`,
  1729	`digest_sha256` (64 lowercase hex, or null where no canonical bytes exist), `retrieval`, `redistribution`,
  1730	`failure_consequence`; strings unless marked. The entry list is section 9's, one entry per document (section 9 lists
  1731	concepts and several RFCs per line; the correspondence is in `formal/dependency-sources/MANIFEST.md`).
  1732	
  1733	**Multi-file sources** [OWNER 2026-10-19, item 15; source formal/dependency-sources/MANIFEST.md]: where the canonical bytes are several files (Bitcoin Core source, git, SHA-1DC),
  1734	`digest_sha256` is the sha256 of a `SHA256SUMS` listing (`sha256sum` format, `<hex>  <relative path>` per file, one
  1735	line per file), which is kept beside the files. Where only an HTML and a PDF form exist (ECMA-262), the digest is of
  1736	the PDF and the HTML digest is in `retrieval`. `digest_sha256` is null when exact source bytes were not obtained,
  1737	with that limitation and its reason stated in `retrieval` (IEEE 754: not fetched). Redistribution restrictions alone
  1738	do not require a null digest. [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-10] *(Was: "A source with no redistributable bytes (IEEE 754)
  1739	has `digest_sha256` null.")*
  1740	
  1741	**Normative, informative, distributed** [OWNER 2026-10-19, item 15; source formal/dependency-sources/MANIFEST.md]: the format has no separate field. The manifest as built marks
  1742	informative entries in free text (the word "informative" in `errata` or `sections`; RFC 9102, RFC 5480, BIP 144),
  1743	and `redistribution` states whether the source is distributed with the verifier, archived with the spec sources, or
  1744	retrieved by reference. Entries not so marked are normative. Only SHA-1DC is distributed with the verifier.
  1745	
  1746	### 15.7 Total check order for the reported reason [OWNER 2026-10-14, G31, G26; r2-F11]
  1747	
  1748	**Numbered check table** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F11; incorporates r2-F02–F10 and r2-F12]. The reported reason is
  1749	the first **failed** row in (row id, tie key) order; if no row failed, the first **unperformable** row. A row whose
  1750	necessary input cannot be obtained because of an earlier row's failure or unperformability is unperformable and
  1751	contributes no reason of its own; the table is ordered so that every such dependency points to an earlier row.
  1752	Rows 11.1–11.4 are evaluated whatever else fails, because they determine T (section 3.4). Slot order is kms, then
  1753	gpg. Candidates are ordered by the G35 tuple (section 7.3), never by their position in a transport array; members
  1754	and missing paths by JCS key order with numeric array indices; heights ascending. The verdict rule (section 3.3) is
  1755	unchanged: INVALID over UNVERIFIABLE, and no early exit before every earlier-priority row and every stable field,
  1756	including T, is settled.
