  1849	### 15.8 Spec and vectors object assembly [OWNER 2026-10-14, G13]
  1850	
  1851	Each object is one or more parts. A part is the ASCII line `tessera-v1-part <path> <length>` LF, then exactly
  1852	`<length>` bytes (decimal, no leading zeros), then LF. `<path>` is repository-relative UTF-8 without spaces. Parts are
  1853	in bytewise-sorted path order; file bytes verbatim as committed. The spec object holds the frozen
  1854	`docs/V1-PROFILE.md` and `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` (plus the branch-registration supplement under the hybrid). The
  1855	vectors object holds every file under `formal/p8/vectors/` and the non-P8 conformance vectors (RES §L). The vectors
  1856	may embed the spec hash; the spec never embeds the vectors' hash.
  1857	
  1858	**Canonical recipe** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F13]. Supersedes the header and inventory sentences above where they
  1859	differ.
  1860	- **Header grammar.** `tessera-v1-part` SP `<path>` SP `<length>` LF, with fixed single literal spaces. `<path>` is an
  1861	  ASCII repository-relative path matching `[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+`, with no empty, `.` or `..` component, no leading
  1862	  slash, and unique within the object; `<length>` matches `0|[1-9][0-9]*`.
  1863	- **Bytes.** Only tracked regular-file **blob bytes** from a named freeze commit: no newline conversion, no symlink
  1864	  following, no generated caches, no working-tree overlay.
  1865	- **Inventory.** The freeze record publishes the exact sorted path inventory of each object, including the non-P8
  1866	  conformance vector files (RES §L names cases, not paths), with an assembler and an independent byte-agreement
  1867	  check.
  1868	- **A9 is a part (owner choice).** The spec object also carries the governing A9 bytes
  1869	  (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md` at its signing commit) as a part, so the object is the complete archival
  1870	  spec; this was chosen over distributing A9 separately with a bound digest.
  1871	- **A10 and the current proof are parts** [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-08]. The spec object also carries, as parts, the
  1872	  signed A10 bytes (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md`, signed by the author's commit `9ade764`; this includes
  1873	  A10.4.2's required custody-procedure contents) and the current repaired `formal/p8/PROOF.md`, each taken at the
  1874	  freeze commit, whose A9 and A10 bytes must equal those at their signing commits. The "complete archival spec"
  1875	  sentence above holds only with these parts. HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT §9's table records the older baseline proof hash
  1876	  (`06910a5f…`); the current proof's SHA-256 is recorded in the freeze record, beside that baseline, outside every
  1877	  included part.
  1878	- **Hashes live outside.** Actual spec, vectors and catalog hashes are recorded in a separate release/freeze record
  1879	  **outside** the frozen spec parts; recording them inside an included file would be self-reference.
  1880	- The part format is a new packaging choice, not an inherited P8 construction or a proven archival format. Once
  1881	  exact catalog hashes exist, runtime hash checks are unique; runtime never parses these objects as policy.
  1882	  *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: "repository-relative UTF-8 without spaces" admitted LF, CR, tab, absolute and
  1883	  dot paths and duplicates; "every file under formal/p8/vectors/" did not select tracked regular files; "the
  1884	  non-P8 conformance vectors (RES §L)" named cases, not files.)*
  1885	
  1886	### 15.9 Release format [OWNER 2026-10-19, §14.2 item 2]
  1887	
  1888	Source: `docs/V1-FREEZE-OPEN.md` §3 (proposal R1–R6) as adjusted by its "Owner decisions" (2026-10-19); A10.4.2
  1889	(rows 34 and 36); the custody draft `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §2–§3. **Frozen at the 30 Oct freeze:** the
  1890	envelope below (archive, release manifest, signature, TEST/production rules, bootstrap rule). **Not frozen:** the
  1891	verifier's code bytes and its inventory, which each release binds (owner decision 1), and the production key,
  1892	ceremony, bootstrap record and rehearsal, which come after the freeze and before the first public verifier release
  1893	(A10.4.2). None of this is a bundle-verifier rule.
  1894	
  1895	**R1. A release is one archive.**
  1896	- One file, `tessera-verifier-v1-<n>.parts` (production) or `tessera-verifier-v1-TEST-<n>.parts` (TEST); `<n>` is the
  1897	  canonical decimal release number.
  1898	- Its bytes are the section 15.8 part format (header grammar, sorted paths). Every part except `RELEASE.json` is
  1899	  copied from the named **source commit** (verbatim tracked regular-file blob bytes). `RELEASE.json` is generated
  1900	  deterministically from those bytes and the declared release metadata; `source_commit` identifies their source
  1901	  commit. The independent assembler reproduces the complete archive, including that generated manifest, and it is
  1902	  compared by hash [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-02] *(Was: "verbatim tracked regular-file blob bytes at one named source
  1903	  commit. Nothing is generated during assembly; the archive is rebuilt from the source commit and compared by
  1904	  hash", which required `RELEASE.json`, a tracked file, to name the commit containing it)* (the section 15.8 assembler and independent byte-agreement check), which is the input the
  1905	  custody procedure's review-and-build step (A10.4.2 C01) needs.
  1906	- Required parts: `RELEASE.json` (R2); `trust.json`, the exact section 15.5 bytes; `catalog.json`, the exact frozen
  1907	  catalog bytes; `release_constants.py` (R2); the dependency lock (R6); the vendored SHA-1DC source with
  1908	  `SHA1DC-PIN.md`; `install.sh`; the relying-party text; the verifier code. No other part carries authority.
  1909	- The archive is unpacked only after R3 succeeds. Scripts run as `bash install.sh`, so no file mode is needed.
  1910	  *(The custody draft's `.tar` is replaced: the part format has one byte string per file set.)*
  1911	
  1912	**R2. The release manifest and how the trust configuration and catalog bind.**
  1913	- `RELEASE.json` is one closed JCS object with exactly these keys: `format` (`"tessera-v1-release/1"`), `namespace`
  1914	  (`"PRODUCTION"` or `"TEST"`), `release` (= `<n>`), `profile` (`"tessera-v1-strict"`), `source_commit` (40 lowercase
  1915	  hex), `trust_config_sha256`, `trust_config_version`, `catalog_sha256`, `freeze_record_sha256`, `parts` (an object
  1916	  mapping every other part's path to the 64-lowercase-hex SHA-256 of its bytes, exactly the archive's other paths),
  1917	  and `release_key_successor` (40 uppercase hex v4 fingerprint, or null). All values are strings except `parts` (an
  1918	  object of strings) and a null successor. `parts` is how a release binds its verifier code (owner decision 1).
  1919	- **Binding equalities**, checked before signing and again by `install.sh`; any failure means no installation:
  1920	  every `parts` entry equals the hash of that part and the path sets are equal; SHA-256(`trust.json`) =
  1921	  `trust_config_sha256` = `release_constants.TRUST_SHA256`; SHA-256(`catalog.json`) = `catalog_sha256` =
  1922	  `release_constants.CATALOG_SHA256`, and `release_constants.CATALOG_BYTES` equals `catalog.json`; the `version`
  1923	  inside `trust.json` = `trust_config_version`; `release_constants.NAMESPACE` = `namespace`. Under `PRODUCTION`,
  1924	  `catalog_sha256` equals the catalog hash in the freeze record whose SHA-256 is `freeze_record_sha256`.
  1925	- Run time is unchanged: a `--trust` file whose hash is not `TRUST_SHA256`, or that fails section 15.5, is
  1926	  `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict. Each new release's `trust.json` passes the release checker (section 5
  1927	  release invariants, EA-12) against the previous release's.
  1928	- **No machine-specific facts in the signed archive.** `release_constants.py` holds only `NAMESPACE`,
  1929	  `TRUST_SHA256`, `CATALOG_SHA256`, `CATALOG_BYTES` and `UNICODE_VERSION` (`"16.0.0"`), all independent of the build
  1930	  machine. Facts about one installation (the interpreter's `unicodedata` file hash, whether SHA-1DC built) are
  1931	  written by `install.sh` into a per-installation record `install.json`, outside the archive and unsigned; the
  1932	  verifier checks them at start (Python 3.14, Unicode 16.0.0, SHA-1DC present, else the existing startup refusals)
  1933	  and reports the SHA-256 of `install.json` as a diagnostic. `install.json` carries no authority. *(Closes the
  1934	  triage's departure: `build_release.py` wrote `UNICODE_MODULE_SHA256` and `SHA1DC_AVAILABLE` from the build
  1935	  machine into the release.)*
  1936	
  1937	**R3. The release signature.**
  1938	- A **detached, binary (unarmored) OpenPGP v4 signature of type 0x00** over the exact archive bytes, file
  1939	  `<archive>.sig`, ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256, satisfying rule O's grammar and policy (section 8). The signing session
  1940	  checks it against rule O before it leaves the session.
  1941	- The release key is **one v4 ECDSA P-256 primary key with no subkeys**, identified by its full v4 fingerprint. A
  1942	  signature by any other key, or by a subkey, authenticates nothing.
  1943	- The signature covers the whole archive, code included. The key never signs a hash written as text.
  1944	- **Acceptance.** Accept only if a trusted acquisition-side checker first validates exactly one unarmored
  1945	  signature against rule O's grammar and section 8 policy, confirms the authenticated primary-key identity (the
  1946	  currently authenticated production key under R5: the bootstrapped key or an authenticated successor), and the
  1947	  pinned `gpgv` verifies the exact archive bytes with a keyring holding **only** that key. Installation then
  1948	  requires R2's equalities. The checker must be available before executing the acquired archive. [OWNER
  1949	  2026-10-19, PR-04, PR-05] *(Was: "Accept iff the pinned `gpgv` exits 0 on `<archive>.sig <archive>` with a keyring
  1950	  holding only the production key whose full fingerprint was obtained by R5"; `gpgv` also exits 0 on a
  1951	  new-format-header signature, on two concatenated signatures and on armored signatures, which rule O rejects.)*
  1952	  The manifest entry `gpgv` pins the upstream GnuPG 2.4.4 source (digest of the tarball) [OWNER 2026-10-19, item 15; source formal/dependency-sources/MANIFEST.md]. It does not certify
  1953	  distribution builds (the owner machine's Ubuntu `2.4.4-2ubuntu17.6` is patched; its bytes differ). A distribution
  1954	  build is acceptable only if it reproduces the upstream behaviour (exit status) on the release-format test vectors.
  1955	  **The pin is upstream GnuPG 2.4.4** [OWNER DECISION 2026-10-23, E9 packet E9-05]. [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-12, superseded] *(Was: "The version is the one observed;
  1956	  R3/R6 have not yet fixed it, so it remains an owner choice", beside an unqualified pin.)*
  1957	- **TEST releases use this same format** with a TEST key over the whole TEST archive, code included. The
  1958	  `tessera-v1-test-release/1` record (two hashes, raw r‖s, SPKI-hash signer) is withdrawn as a release: it did not
  1959	  cover the verifier code. It survives only as the section 3.5 instrument interface for the conformance and
  1960	  differential harnesses, which is never a release and which an installed verifier refuses (R4). *(Closes the
  1961	  triage's departure on TEST signature coverage.)*
  1962	
  1963	**R4. Production is the default; TEST is explicit.**
  1964	- Every release before the first public verifier release is a **TEST release**: `namespace` `"TEST"`, a `TEST` file
  1965	  name, signed by a TEST release key in the R3 format. TEST keys are throwaway v4 P-256 keys and may live on the
  1966	  operational computer. A TEST public key is never published as the release key, never named in the freeze record,
  1967	  the bootstrap record or relying-party text, and never named in any `release_key_successor` (A10 row 36).
  1968	- The production key signs only `"PRODUCTION"`; a TEST key signs only `"TEST"`. The signing session checks this.
  1969	- **Production refusal is by key, not by label.** A TEST signature fails R3 because the production keyring holds only
  1970	  the production key. The label prevents honest mistakes; it is not a security check.
  1971	- **Mode comes from the installed archive, never from the command line.** There is no `--production` flag. An
  1972	  installed verifier whose `NAMESPACE` is not exactly `"TEST"` is a production verifier: it refuses
  1973	  `--test-release`, `--test-release-key` and every `TESSERA_V1_TEST_*` variable with `TEST_AUTHORITY_REFUSED` and
  1974	  no verdict, whatever else the command line or environment says. Omitting a flag can therefore never select TEST.
  1975	- **The explicit TEST provisioning path** is one of: (a) an installed TEST archive (`NAMESPACE = "TEST"`),
  1976	  acquired by R3 against a TEST keyring the operator names explicitly; or (b) the section 3.5 instrument interface,
  1977	  usable only from a source checkout with no installed release, where the TEST release, TEST key and catalog must
  1978	  all be supplied; with none supplied it refuses (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`). *(Closes the triage's departure:
  1979	  production mode was a CLI flag, so an invocation without it accepted TEST provisioning.)*
  1980	- **Rehearsal (A10 row 36), before the first public verifier release:** an otherwise correct archive signed only by
  1981	  a TEST key is refused by the R3/R5 acquisition procedure, including when the TEST public key is offered as the
  1982	  key; and an installed production verifier refuses the section 3.5 interface.
  1983	
  1984	**R5. Out-of-band authentication of the production key** (owner decision 2).
  1985	- The relying party's root of trust is the production key's **full v4 fingerprint**, obtained before and apart from
  1986	  any release. A key or fingerprint that arrives with a release, on the same page as a release, or from the
  1987	  operational environment authenticates nothing. The operational environment is assessed by actual roles,
  1988	  credentials and release authorization, not by provider names; it includes at least the operational computer
  1989	  (the NUC), this repository, DNS, the issuing AWS account and the GCP project that holds the slot-2 key, and
  1990	  another provider's name does not by itself establish independence [AUTHOR COMMENT 2026-10-23, E9 row 6]. Such a
  1991	  key or fingerprint authenticates nothing, except a
  1992	  successor fingerprint named by a conforming release verified under the currently authenticated key (Rotation,
  1993	  below). [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-05]
  1994	- **Frozen rule:** the fingerprint is published through **at least two channels independent of the operational
  1995	  environment**, named in the custody record before the first public verifier release (A10.4.2). The channels are
  1996	  the author's to name. *(Owner recommendation for then, not frozen: a Zenodo record under the author's ORCID plus
  1997	  one offline printed copy.)* A relying party who did not obtain the fingerprint this way relies on authenticated
  1998	  first acquisition as an assumption, which the relying-party text states (A10.4.2: "does not protect a user who …
  1999	  acquires a substituted initial key").
  2000	- The fingerprint is recorded in the dated **first-production-release bootstrap record**, which names the freeze
  2001	  record by hash and the production key, and is published and OpenTimestamps-stamped. Publication and stamping give
  2002	  a dated public record; they are not authentication. The freeze record does not contain the fingerprint.
  2003	- **Initial bootstrap and rotation** [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-05]. Initial bootstrap authenticates the first
  2004	  production key. A successor is authenticated by a conforming release verified under the currently authenticated
  2005	  key and naming the successor fingerprint in `release_key_successor`; subsequently acquired successor key material
  2006	  must match that fingerprint. The next release is signed by the successor.
  2007	- **Loss or compromise.** V1 has no assured recovery. New releases stop. Loss recovery starts a separately
  2008	  authenticated release lineage, with new out-of-band publication under this rule and a new dated bootstrap record,
  2009	  and does not establish continuity with the old lineage. Genuine old releases stay available. [OWNER 2026-10-19,
  2010	  PR-05] *(Was: "a replacement key needs new out-of-band publication under this rule and a new dated bootstrap
  2011	  record", which read as a second way to introduce a key in the same lineage.)*
  2012	
  2013	**R6. Dependency pinning.**
  2014	- The archive carries a lock with exact versions **and SHA-256 hashes** for every Python package, installed with
  2015	  hash checking required; wheels are retrieved at install time, not distributed.
  2016	- Python 3.14 with Unicode 16.0.0 tables is checked at install and at start; SHA-1DC is built from the vendored,
  2017	  digest-checked source.
  2018	- The relying party's `gpgv` version and the OpenPGP standard it implements are dependency-manifest entries
  2019	  (section 9; section 14.2 item 15). Verification is offline after installation; installation is not.
