    67	**Part 2** has three new items. The owner and Codex recommended them. Codex is a second AI system that reviews this
    68	work. The author accepted all three on 18 October 2026. They add new mechanism to V1.
    69	
    70	**UTC: what U is.** UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) is the standard measure of real time. Amendment 9 states no
    71	UTC time.
    72	
    73	- Under A-UTC, U is a UTC upper bound: the latest chain time of the six confirming blocks, plus two hours.
    74	- The claim is conditional: if A-UTC is true, the content and both signatures existed before U.
    75	
    76	**UTC: the assumption A-UTC.** A miner is a party that makes Bitcoin blocks.
    77	
    78	- A-UTC says: at least one of those six blocks states a chain time no more than two hours before its real
    79	  production time.
    80	- A-UTC is false only if the miners of all six blocks state chain times more than two hours early.
    81	- Nothing in Bitcoin or in the verifier makes sure that A-UTC is true.
    82	- The author's signature accepts A-UTC as an assumption. The signature does not show that miners make A-UTC true.
    83	
    84	**UTC: the evidence.**
    85	
    86	- The retained measurements cover 2015 to 2026. In each complete set of six, one recorded arrival was no later
    87	  than chain time plus 1065 seconds.
    88	- The dataset rejects arrival times more than two hours from the chain time. It cannot show failures beyond that
    89	  limit.
    90	- Observer clocks can also be wrong. These measurements do not show that A-UTC was true.
    91	
    92	**UTC: the output.**
    93	
    94	- The verifier reports U with its assumption identifier, `A-UTC-7200-6`, in `utc_assessment`.
    95	- U, conditional on A-UTC, is outside the six fields that later verifiers must reproduce. U does not change the
    96	  verdict.
    97	- Each statement of U must give the assumption A-UTC next to it.
    98	
    99	**UTC: the year 2106.** In 2106 the Bitcoin time field reaches its maximum. The calculation of U, whose UTC claim
   100	depends on A-UTC, stays correct for the saved headers of older blocks. Bitcoin, not V1, must define the times of
   101	blocks after that point.
   102	
   103	**Key substitution: the problem.** An attacker with both authority-record credentials can make authority records
   104	that name the attacker's own keys. Without a new mechanism, receipts under those keys can get VALID.
   105	
   106	**Key substitution: option (e).**
   107	
   108	- Option (e) adds a list of the service keys to the trust file. The release key authenticates the trust file.
   109	- The verifier gives INVALID or CANNOT VERIFY for a receipt whose keys are not on the list.
   110	- The release-key holder must not add keys to the list only because the two authority records name them.
   111	- The author chose option (e). He did not choose option (a), which has no list, or options (b) to (d).
   112	
   113	**Key substitution: the limits.**
   114	
   115	- The protection applies only to a user whose verifier release is authentic and comes from an honest release-key
   116	  holder.
   117	- It stops those users from accepting such receipts.
   118	- It does not make sure that anybody finds or publishes the attack.
   119	- An attacker who also has the release key can make a release that lists the attacker's keys.
   120	
   121	**Key substitution: time.** A release must list the exact keys and window before the receipt's Bitcoin anchor. To
   122	check this after release-key theft, keep the first listing release and its proof, and authenticate the Bitcoin
   123	history for both anchors independently of the stolen key. The release-key fingerprint alone cannot identify a forged
   124	release history. Keep this evidence together with the receipt.
   125	
   126	**Release-key custody: the rule.** AWS means Amazon Web Services, the provider of the service's cloud account.
   127	Amendment 9 already requires these access limits:
   128	
   129	- The DNS credential and the repository key must not give access to the release key.
   130	- Access to the AWS account must not give access to the release key.
   131	- Hardware or offline software for the release key is acceptable only with a written procedure.
   132	
   133	The procedure has these contents:
   134	
   135	- The procedure must say who authorizes each release.
   136	- The procedure must say where the signing occurs.
   137	- The procedure must say how backups stay outside the systems that operate the service.
   138	- The procedure must say how a separate system checks each release before the signing.
   139	- The procedure must say how files move into and out of the signing system.
   140	
   141	**Release-key custody: when.**
   142	
   143	- At the freeze, the rule, the release format and the contents of the procedure must be fixed.
   144	- After the freeze, the owner builds and tests the signing system.
   145	- A reviewer must check the procedure before the first public verifier release.
   146	- The target for that release is before 20 November 2026.
   147	
   148	**Release-key custody: test releases.** Each early release uses a separate test release key. Production checks
   149	never accept this key. Users must authenticate the production release key before they rely on a release. The
   150	service must not offer an early release for real use. A test must show that production checks refuse a release
   151	with only a test signature.
   152	
   153	**Release-key custody: risks that the owner names.**
   154	
   155	- The schedule risk moves later. The owner must start the preparation early.
   156	- The rules do not change after the freeze, because the release format is fixed at the freeze.
   157	- Production checks refuse an early release, because it has only a test signature.
   158	- The author accepts the use of his time in November. He wrote: "(4) is acceptable to me".
   159	- The service does not offer an early release for real use.
   160	
   161	**Release-key custody: if independence fails.** The purchase of hardware alone does not make the key independent.
   162	If the owner cannot show independence before the first public verifier release, the author gets a NARROW-MODEL. That
   163	model is a smaller protection against an attacker with authority-record credentials. The removal of the key list
   164	does not repair this.
   165	
   166	Two other mechanisms are recorded as candidates only: time-stamp authorities and a witnessed public log. This
   167	amendment does not require anybody to make them.
   168	
   169	Each change has a kind:
   170	
   171	- CLARIFY: the meaning does not change.
   172	- NARROW-CLAIM: the claim becomes smaller, so that it states only what the checks establish.
   173	- CORRECT: the wording disagreed with a registered rule or model. The correction follows that rule or model
   174	  without reducing the claim.
   175	- BROADEN: the claim becomes larger. It adds a mechanism or a stated assumption.
   176	- GATE: the change adds or changes a prerequisite, a trigger, or a cut order. The row states any scope change that
   177	  follows.
