   358	## A10.2 The window establishes two comparisons, not issuance "inside" it — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A02)
   359	
   360	A9's opening says the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window" (:36), and guarantee 2's heading says
   361	"Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time" (:441). The C4 verifier rule (:419-421) checks
   362	only two comparisons, and says "T itself is not compared with not-before".
   363	
   364	**Reading adopted.** The verifier establishes exactly two inclusive comparisons, with no margin:
   365	- the declared issue time is not earlier than `issuance-not-before`;
   366	- the verified chain-time T is not later than `issuance-not-after`.
   367	
   368	It does not establish that T is not earlier than not-before, that the declared issue time is not later than
   369	not-after, or that any signing act occurred inside the interval. The review's two arithmetic witnesses satisfy the
   370	two window comparisons and all three A2 temporal comparisons. They are not executed receipt vectors or evidence
   371	that an honest issuer ships those values. The checks do not change. The issuer invariant (:422, an attempt whose T
   372	would exceed not-after is not shipped) stays an issuer duty, not a verifier comparison. "Issuance window" stays the
   373	name of the record's two values. The changed sentences are rows 10–12 of A10.7.
   374	
   375	## A10.3 Reorganization depth, at k = 6 and `DepthK = k − 1` — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A03)
   376	
   377	A9 excludes "Bitcoin history deeper than k" (:49-50) and calls "Reorganization deeper than k" a residual (C5,
   378	:144). A9 also imports k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1` (:105) and A2.1's count, in which the including block is the
   379	first confirmation.
   380	
   381	**Definition.** The depth of a reorganization is the number of blocks it removes from the tip of the chain. A
   382	reorganization of depth d removes every block that has d or fewer confirmations when it occurs. At k = 6
   383	confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block; P5c's `depth` counts those five (`depth = c − 1`, so
   384	`DepthK = 5`).
   385	
   386	**Correction.** The registered assumption is that anchors with k or more confirmations are permanent (A1.6:
   387	"anchors at depth ≥ k are treated as permanent"). P5c's `Reorg` action can orphan an anchor only while
   388	`depth < DepthK`, that is, at five or fewer confirmations. So a reorganization of depth exactly k, which removes an
   389	anchor with k confirmations, is outside both. A9's "deeper than k" read literally leaves that case inside the
   390	claim, with no evidence. The excluded residual is therefore **a reorganization of depth k or more**. This narrows
   391	A9's literal prose claim at equality. It changes neither the model nor its permanence assumption, and withdraws no
   392	checked model result. This does not affect C4's chain-order gap (`g + 5 < r`), which the review found correct. The
   393	changed sentences are rows 13–14 of A10.7.
   394	
   395	## A10.4 Authority capture: honest signed material protected; publication remains an issuer duty; key substitution — AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-18: option (e) (recommended by the owner; option (e) also by Codex), NARROW-CLAIM and BROADEN (review A04)
   396	
   397	A9's adversary sentence includes "anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays
   398	uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)" (:41-42). It names no set of receipts and no object for "signing
   399	capability". A8.3 (:72-76) bounds the tested property: under capture, "a captor cannot reattribute honest frames,
   400	alter honest signer sets, or carry honest signatures onto other bytes or keys". That property concerns honest
   401	service material, not only receipts issued before disclosure; the capture fixtures disclose the credentials before
